---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Cube from '../components/Cube.astro';
import { DOC, LICENSE_URL, REPO_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'Why open source | MetricMine';
const description =
  'MetricMine is Apache-2.0 because the method is the proof: agents propose, a person approves, and the approval is a pull request anyone can read. No pricing, no hosted tier, no exceptions but the name and the logo.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/why-open-source/">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Why open source" title="The method is the proof, and proof needs readers.">
    Every claim on this site points at a file in a public repository. That is
    not a distribution choice made after the fact. It is the reason the
    project can say what it says.
  </PageHero>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Four reasons</p>
        <h2>Why the code is open.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="cards two">
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>The approval is a pull request.</h3>
          <p>
            Agents propose, humans approve, and the approval is a merged pull
            request with a named person on it. That sentence only means
            something if anyone can read the pull requests, the register that
            explains them, and the findings that measured them. They can, all
            of it, from the first commit.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>Reproducibility needs strangers.</h3>
          <p>
            A clone of main at any tag gets a working demo: <code>make doctor</code>,
            then <code>make demo</code>, keyless. Two machines that pull the same
            commit build the same gold, and the demo digest matched across a
            Linux sandbox, a Mac, and a Windows runner. Those are claims a stranger can falsify,
            which is what makes them worth making.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>The parts are open, so the whole is.</h3>
          <p>
            ODCS is a Linux Foundation standard. dbt Core, DuckDB, PyAirbyte,
            and the MCP SDK are open source. A reference implementation of how
            they fit together would be a strange thing to close.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <Cube />
          <h3>Apache-2.0, and nothing behind it.</h3>
          <p>
            There is no pricing, no hosted tier, and no service. The demo runs
            on your machine and the only database artifact the project
            publishes is the export attached to a release. The stable line
            changes only through the decision register, and every change is a
            reviewed pull request.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">What open source means here</p>
        <h2>A stable line, kept honestly.</h2>
        <p>
          The stable line began at v1.0.0. The pipeline, the engine, the
          serving layer, the two proposers, and the gates change only through
          the decision register; experiments live on branches or under a
          documented experiments folder and end in written findings; a clone
          of main at any tag gets a working demo. That promise is written on
          every release surface and in the coding agent's own instructions.
        </p>
        <p>
          It does not mean a service level. There is no support commitment,
          no uptime, and no performance figure, and the non-goals that keep it
          that way are written into the README: multi-tenancy, auth, billing,
          any UI beyond the read-only demo, streaming, orchestration
          platforms, autonomous multi-step agents, more than one connector
          type, and production service levels.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The one exception</p>
        <h2>The name and the logo.</h2>
        <p>
          The MetricMine name and the MetricMine logo are not covered by the
          Apache-2.0 license. Forks and derivative works may not use them in a
          way that suggests endorsement by, or affiliation with, this project.
          Everything else in the repository is yours to use under the license
          in <a href={LICENSE_URL}>LICENSE</a>, with the provenance statement
          in <a href={DOC.notice}>NOTICE</a> beside it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The website and the brand system live in{' '}
          <a href={DOC.websiteRepo}>their own repository</a>: MIT for the
          site's code, all rights reserved for the brand assets, and the
          normative <a href={DOC.brandStandards}>brand standard</a> beside
          them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Taking part</p>
        <h2>Small reviewed pull requests, contracts never weakened.</h2>
        <p>
          Contributions are welcome under the conventions in{' '}
          <a href={DOC.contributing}>CONTRIBUTING.md</a>: small reviewed pull
          requests, a contract never weakened to pass a gate, a version bump on
          every contract change, and a sign-off on every commit, which the DCO
          check enforces. Everyone taking part follows the{' '}
          <a href={DOC.conduct}>code of conduct</a>. Vulnerabilities go through{' '}
          <a href={DOC.security}>SECURITY.md</a>, never a public issue.
        </p>
        <p>
          The issue forms know the project's shapes: a bug, a contract change,
          and a finding. A finding is the project's favorite kind of issue,
          because a measurement that contradicts the plan is how the plan
          improves, and fifty-three of them are on record already.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Next</p>
      <h2>Read it, run it, or file a finding.</h2>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={REPO_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/get-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .band {
      background: var(--mm-bg-raised);
      border-block: 1px solid var(--mm-border);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
